# <%= projectName %> — CANON

> Process-level rules that sit below invariants (INV-NN) but above individual
> implementation decisions in the Authority Hierarchy. Every implementation
> of an open issue must cite the CANON-NN IDs that apply.

> **Violation protocol:** STOP → REFUSE → cite CANON-NN (same as INV-NN).

---

## How to read this file

Each entry is a triplet:

1. **Rule** — the canonical statement (what MUST happen).
2. **Why** — the failure mode the rule prevents.
3. **Enforcement** — the gate, hook, or review step that catches violations.

When a CANON entry graduates to a machine check it is promoted into a
machine-enforced INV-NN. The CANON-NN entry stays as historical record
with a `Promoted to: INV-NN` footer.

---

## How to add a new CANON

1. Append `## CANON-NN` at the end of this file (increment N).
2. Use the triplet structure above. Cite the issue that prompted the rule.
3. If you add a gate or hook to enforce it, also add an `INV-NN` entry to
   `GLOBAL_INVARIANTS.md` and link them with `Promoted to: INV-NN`.

---

## CANON-01 — Dual-sided declination

**Rule:** Every governance decision applies to both the framework (self) and the
generated project output (scaffold). A self-only rule with no scaffold mirror
is incomplete; a scaffold-only rule that the framework itself bypasses is
unprincipled. Plans must address both tracks or explicitly justify a single-track scope.

**Why:** Asymmetric enforcement erodes trust. If the framework enforces rule X
for users but not for itself, the rule reads as bureaucracy; if it enforces
for itself but not for users, generated projects inherit weaker guarantees.

**Enforcement:** Prose — checked at PR review for every governance change.

---

## CANON-02 — Proven cell ⇒ gated step

**Rule:** Any language × tool × archetype × level cell promoted to `proven`
in the compatibility matrix MUST have a corresponding gate step wired into
the level's `check-all.mjs` invocation.

**Why:** A `proven` claim without a gate is paper certification.

**Enforcement:** Matrix integrity tests assert that every `proven` cell
has a matching gate step.

---

## CANON-03 — Proven cell ⇒ fixture

**Rule:** Any language with at least one `proven` cell in the matrix MUST
have at least one fixture project under `__tests__/fixtures/real-projects/`
exercising that language end-to-end.

**Why:** A proven claim without a fixture is hypothesis, not evidence.

**Enforcement:** L1 gate — scripts/check-matrix-fixtures.mjs.

---

## CANON-04 — Every .ejs has a render test

**Rule:** Every `.ejs` template under `src/templates/` MUST have at least one
test that renders the template against a realistic config and asserts the
absence of EJS leak markers (`<%`, `%>`) in the output.

**Why:** EJS syntax errors and missing variables silently produce malformed
output. Render tests are the cheapest way to catch them.

**Enforcement:** L1 gate — render coverage check.

---

## CANON-05 — Every generator has a unit test

**Rule:** Every file under `src/generators/` MUST have a corresponding unit
test exercising at least its happy path and one error path.

**Why:** Generators emit governance artifacts. An untested generator can
silently corrupt every downstream project.

**Enforcement:** L1 gate — generator-coverage check.

---

## CANON-06 — Every CLI command has a test

**Rule:** Every file under `src/commands/` MUST have a test that invokes the
command end-to-end with realistic arguments and asserts exit code + stdout.

**Why:** CLI commands are the user-facing contract. Unverified commands break
silently in users' hands.

**Enforcement:** L1 gate — command-test coverage check.

---

## CANON-07 — Generated scripts must be executed in tests

**Rule:** When a generator emits an executable script (`.sh`, `.mjs`, `.py`,
etc.) into a target project, the test for that generator MUST execute the
emitted script against a fixture and assert at least the exit code.

**Why:** Generating a syntactically-valid but semantically-broken script
silently breaks every consumer.

**Enforcement:** Prose — checked at PR review for any generator that emits
a script artifact.

---

## CANON-08 — Catalog ↔ AGENTS.md parity

**Rule:** Every invariant in `src/invariants/catalog.ts` MUST appear in
`AGENTS.md` §Invariants with matching ID and title. Drift between the two
breaks plan validation.

**Why:** AGENTS.md is the human-readable contract; the catalog is the
machine-readable one. They must agree.

**Enforcement:** L1 gate — catalog/AGENTS.md parity check.

---

## CANON-09 — Invariant enforcement claim = wired gate step

**Rule:** Every invariant whose `enforcement` field references a script MUST
have that script wired into `check-all.mjs` at the declared level.

**Why:** An invariant that claims gate enforcement but is not actually wired
is paper enforcement.

**Enforcement:** L1 gate — invariant-enforcement-wired check.

---

## CANON-10 — Every active hook is documented in .claude/CLAUDE.md

**Rule:** Every entry under `.claude/settings.json` `hooks:` MUST appear in
`.claude/CLAUDE.md` §Hooks with its event, purpose, and any bypass mechanism.

**Why:** Undocumented hooks surprise contributors and make debugging painful.

**Enforcement:** Prose — checked at PR review when settings.json changes.

---

## CANON-11 — Every file-emitting generator has a brownfield test

**Rule:** Every generator that writes files into a target project MUST have
a test that runs against a pre-existing target directory and asserts safe
behaviour (idempotent re-runs, no clobber of user files, etc.).

**Why:** Greenfield-only tests miss the real-world case where users run
the tool against an existing repo. Brownfield testing catches data-loss bugs.

**Enforcement:** L1 gate — brownfield coverage check.

---

## CANON-12 — Invariant-12 applies to <%= projectName %>'s own source

**Rule:** The "no direct child_process" rule from INV-12 applies to this
project's own `src/` tree as well as to generated targets. All process
spawning must go through the approved helper (see GLOBAL_INVARIANTS.md INV-12).

**Why:** Self-application is the proof that the rule is liveable. If the
framework bypasses its own invariant, users will too.

**Enforcement:** L1 gate — no-direct-spawn hook + check.

---

## CANON-13 — EJS conditionals preserved on every template edit

**Rule:** When editing or merging EJS templates, all governance-level `if`
guards and inline value interpolations MUST be preserved. Every
template change MUST be tested across the supported stack × governance
matrix.

**Why:** EJS conditionals are load-bearing: collapsing an L3-only verifier
block into an L1 path silently breaks all L1/L2 generated projects.

**Enforcement:** Cross-product render tests must stay green on every
template change.

---

## CANON-14 — Self-config ⊇ template at equal governance level

**Rule:** Every hook this project emits for target projects at governance
level L MUST also run in its own `.claude/settings.json` if its own declared
governance level is ≥ L.

**Why:** A framework that does not eat its own dogfood at the same level
it ships cannot credibly claim the rule is liveable.

**Enforcement:** Prose — diff the template against the materialised file
on every hook addition.

---

## CANON-15 — Boundary/security templates require a wired gate step

**Rule:** Emitting a linter config, security scanner config, or
architecture-boundary config file is not sufficient. The corresponding
gate step MUST also appear in `check-all.mjs` at the appropriate level.

**Why:** A config file without a gate invocation is a paper rule. No CI
runner will enforce it unless the gate calls it.

**Enforcement:** Prose — checked at PR review for any new template that
emits a tool config file.

---

## CANON-16 — Refactor-first before creating new source files

**Rule:** Before creating any new file under `src/`, a plan MUST include
an "Existing Code Survey" section documenting: what was grepped for, what
similar code was found, and whether a refactor was chosen or rejected
(with reason).

**Why:** Unchecked file creation is the primary source of near-duplicate
abstractions, bloated generator counts, and redundant templates. A senior
developer asks "does this already exist?" before building.

**Enforcement:** Pre-edit plan-anchor hook hard-blocks `Write` to any new
`src/` file if the active plan lacks a valid `## Existing Code Survey`
block for that file.

<% if (governanceLevel === 'L3') { -%>
**L3 note:** Survey decisions are reviewed against `jscpd` duplication
findings on every PR; near-duplicate functions added with a `new file
justified` decision MUST be refactored or the decision MUST be revised.

<% } -%>

