## CANON-17 — Filesystem errno translation

**Rule:** Every direct `fs.*` failure handler in `src/` MUST translate raw
NodeJS errno codes (ENOENT, EACCES, EPERM, EBUSY, EMFILE, ENOSPC) into a
human-readable error with a hint. Raw NodeJS stack traces or bare errno
strings MUST NOT leak to user-facing stdout/stderr from CLI commands.

**Why:** Errno strings are opaque to users; "ENOENT" tells the user nothing
about what is missing or how to fix it.

**Enforcement:** Prose — checked at PR review for any code path that
invokes `fs.*` directly.

---

## CANON-18 — Every workflow EJS template edit tested across the matrix

**Rule:** When adding or modifying any workflow template under
`src/templates/github/workflows/*.ejs`, the test suite MUST render the
template for the full stack × governance matrix and assert `actionlint`
passes on every rendered output.

**Why:** Workflow templates are the primary CI governance artifact emitted
by the framework. A syntax error or missing interpolation variable
silently breaks generated CI for any project using that stack or level.

**Enforcement:** Cross-product render tests extended with workflow
assertions.

---

## CANON-19 — Composite action edits require release workflow re-validation

**Rule:** When adding or modifying any composite action template
(`.github/actions/<name>/action.yml.ejs`), the release workflow template
MUST be re-rendered and validated for all archetype buckets.

**Why:** Composite actions are called from multiple publish job paths.
A breaking input rename or missing output silently breaks release signing
for every archetype that references the action.

**Enforcement:** Prose — checked at PR review for any composite action
template edit.

---

## CANON-20 — Governance threshold changes require cross-product fixture update

**Rule:** When editing the CI tier threshold matrix, the affected workflow
templates MUST be re-rendered and their output verified to contain the
updated threshold values. Fixture snapshots MUST be regenerated.

**Why:** Threshold values flow from the threshold matrix through EJS
interpolation into generated workflow YAML. A change that does not
propagate through fixtures creates a silent divergence between what the
spec promises and what generated projects receive.

**Enforcement:** Prose — checked at PR review when the threshold matrix
changes.

---

## CANON-21 — Aggregate, don't proliferate

**Rule:** Before creating any new `scripts/check-*.mjs` gate, the plan MUST
include a written justification explaining why the new logic cannot fold
into an existing sibling script as a `--mode` flag or additional rule. The
justification MUST cite concrete grep evidence of which sibling scripts
were considered and why each was rejected. New scripts added without this
justification block MUST carry a `// CATALOG:` marker (≥3 contiguous
header comment lines) declaring what the script aggregates and why a new
file is preferable to extending an existing one.

**Why:** The `scripts/check-*.mjs` namespace is the operational surface
this project presents to its own developers and to CI. Unchecked growth
produces a long undifferentiated list of single-purpose scripts that
nobody can hold in their head, each with its own argument parsing,
baseline format, and exit-code conventions. Aggregating related checks
into a small number of multi-mode scripts keeps the catalog learnable,
reduces near-duplicate code, and forces explicit thinking about cohesion
before file creation.

**Enforcement:** `scripts/check-script-cohesion.mjs` (L2 gate) — reads
`scripts/data/script-catalog-baseline.json`, hard-fails when a script
outside the baseline lacks a `// CATALOG:` header block; emits a warning
when the total script count exceeds the baseline by more than 5.

